  "worked_solution": "At $x=4,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\sqrt{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(4)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x) & =g(4)=4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ 3 & =4a \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
